WEBVTT

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:53.360
I'm Heston Blumenthal and for the last eight weeks I've been perfecting eight classic dishes, but now I attempt to take on what, for many people, is the biggest menu of the year - Christmas dinner.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:01:02.320
This is the big one, the meal we all look forward to, and I'm going to stop at nothing to get it right.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:10.400
This is my chance to incorporate all of my Christmas memories into one glorious meal.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:29.640
My restaurant has three Michelin stars and has been voted one of the best in the world and now I'm going to bring my multi-sensory approach to cooking to deliver a once-in-a-lifetime Christmas meal, unlike anything ever seen.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:39.160
'I'll be visiting Santa's little helper to try and make reindeer-milk ice cream.'

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:41.480
I can't see the teat.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:47.120
'I lovingly rear two special geese to capture a tradition of Christmas past.'

00:01:50.880 --> 00:02:01.200
'And I'll be travelling to the Middle East in the footsteps of the Three Wise Men 'to create a dish from gold, frankincense and myrrh.'

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.960
It's not just the meal that's going to be special.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:13.280
I'm also going to create a Christmas dining room that's out of this world to give my diners a Christmas experience like no other.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:25.200
Christmas wouldn't be Christmas without friends, so I've invited six special guests to enjoy this magical meal and they've no idea what I've got in store for them.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:33.320
'This is my perfect Christmas dinner.'

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:37.440
If you asked people to name three...

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:45.840
Five of the most memorable meals they've ever had, the food will be good, but most of the time it will be the setting, the company, the occasion.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:55.360
There will be other things - the environment provides those sensory triggers, the memories, that make it so wonderful.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:59.160
'First on the agenda was creating the perfect setting for my meal.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:06.040
'My idea was to turn a local car park into an incredible Christmas dining room.'

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:07.760
A stone entrance.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:13.120
The guests will come in through here and then they walk into what is basically a drawing room.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:15.880
One of the panels will actually be a false wall.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.960
This is a forest of Christmas trees.

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.320
The table will be there, and the chandelier.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:22.240
Sounds good.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.440
A gloves-off Christmas dinner.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:30.040
So, with my dining room ready, it was time for my guests to arrive.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:36.000
I've invited six special guests, who all love their food, to come and share this feast.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.600
I'm looking forward to it enormously.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:45.280
We wouldn't be here if we weren't, but Heston is probably the most exciting chef in Britain, if not the world.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:53.160
It's this thing with snail porridge and things that taste different and steam and nitrogen and gas, and all that stuff.

00:03:53.240 --> 00:04:01.280
I know somebody who's been here, and had seafood and they put... headphones on you and you had the sound of the sea.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:04.080
I think we'll be wearing goblin hats.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:07.960
It'll be something stupid - you'll put the food in your mouth and go, "This has no taste!"

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.920
Then he'll stand behind you and shake your head violently and then you go, "Oh, yes, I get it now.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:11.320
Bergamot!"

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:17.240
You know, the satsuma bomb goes off!

00:04:16.279 --> 00:04:18.640
Exactly.

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:28.000
The drawing room on this set was the opportunity for me to generate some excitement.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:29.520
Oh!

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:30.880
Please help yourself, come in.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.800
Don't do this at home!

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:40.120
We actually made what looked like glass Christmas tree decorations and filled it with salmon mousse, but it wasn't glass.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:44.920
It's very good.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.240
Salmon in a light bulb!

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:57.520
That's extraordinary.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:06.720
Next for my guests was the very traditional Christmas treat of mulled wine, but with a little extra magic.

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:09.400
Oh, my God!

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:13.960
Almost right down the middle, one half is hot and the other half is cold.

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:14.560
If I can just...

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:15.200
How do you do that?!

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:18.280
How have they halved it?

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:21.800
Half of it is hot and half of it is quite cold.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:22.560
Exactly.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:28.320
One half of your mouth is frozen while the other is warm.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:38.400
It took me a very long time to develop that to the level where I thought it was good enough to serve, but I can't disclose the secret because it would lose the magic.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:43.840
I don't think I've ever drunk anything quite so ridiculous in my life!

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:52.320
Now it's a mandarin mousse - egg white, vodka and mandarin - that when poached in liquid nitrogen becomes a meringue-like taste explosion.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:06:03.640
It's been dusted with dried mandarin powder and then there's a little spritz, a little spray of thyme oil in the air and if you get the whole thing in your mouth in one go, you get two jets of vapour coming out of your nostrils, like a dragon!

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:09.160
There's steam coming out of your nose.

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:13.920
THEY LAUGH Like a bull!

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:17.720
Oh, my God, that was amazing.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:25.160
Six veteran media types and we're all reduced to eight-year-old children.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:27.640
Which was exactly the effect I wanted to create.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:33.400
Then, through a secret panel, it was time to reveal my Christmas dining room.

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:41.000
Look at the snow!

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:51.640
It's Narnia!

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:02.440
A first for some.

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:05.200
It's so beautiful.

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:13.000
It was like entering some kind of winter wonderland.

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:13.800
Um...it just looked glorious.

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:14.120
Oh, look at that.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:14.360
Stunning.

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:27.920
You walk through this... entrance hall that's been created into this outdoor...

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:31.400
Disneyland of Christmas-ness!

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:31.720
Oh, look at this!

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:37.840
If this is the amount of work they put into the trees, what the hell is the food going to taste like?

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:41.880
So to the meal and my first course.

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:55.400
And there's three things that are key to that.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:02.480
Gold, frankincense and myrrh, which took me to Oman.

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:14.520
I'd come to the Middle East on the trail of the Three Wise Men.

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:21.720
My ambition was to track down 2,000-year-old frankincense from the time of Christ and include it in my meal.

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:25.680
To do this, I had to travel to the place where the Wise Men themselves might have found it.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:32.480
A legendary frankincense trading post - the lost city of Ubar.

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:39.919
This is satellite imagery from the late '80s, which led to the discovery of the lost city of Ubar.

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:53.640
The thing that really enabled the archaeologists to find this city was these - these camel tracks, and where they meet, this triangle, that was Ubar.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:58.400
And we believe this is where the Three Wise Men would have actually passed through.

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:00.120
Now, these camel tracks have turned into a kind of road and we're on one.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:06.160
And I can't believe it!

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:07.120
There's a camel.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:13.480
At least we knew we were on the right route.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:19.760
A few miles down the road, we found Ubar and one of the men responsible for its discovery - archaeologist Yuri Zarins.

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:29.880
The site itself probably goes back to 200-300 BC, but the actual occupation goes back to 5,000 BC.

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:31.360
People have been here for the last 7,000 years.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:35.760
Biblical research has shown that the Wise Men came from parts of Saudi Arabia.

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:39.400
They did bring frankincense and myrrh to the baby Jesus.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:43.160
That road would've come through here, straight up to the Levantine coast.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:48.480
We can see the outline of the town itself, the main settlement.

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:50.600
Merchants were attached to each one of these.

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:57.680
They'd be selling goods - frankincense and myrrh etc. So we're in a market stall?

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:57.920
It's a market stall.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:09:59.680
That's exactly what it is.

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:08.360
'At this site, they have excavated biblical frankincense, 'but, sadly, it was far too valuable and rare for me to use in a meal.

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:11.880
'Instead, I was going to have to find and harvest my own.'

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:17.880
Is there anywhere relatively near here I can actually tap into a frankincense tree?

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:18.480
Across the river.

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:21.680
It's only 50km to where the frankincense trees grow.

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:26.800
This is when they start to harvest, at the end of the monsoon season.

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:33.480
Frankincense was, and is, used for a myriad of purposes, from burning at funerals to medicine.

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:42.400
In ancient times, this crystallised tree sap was one of the most expensive things on earth and would have been brought by the Wise Men as a highly symbolic gift.

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:47.920
Finally, my quest led me to the harvesting fields, where the locals still guard the valuable sap jealously.

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:53.280
Have you ever used frankincense in cooking?

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:53.680
No.

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:55.080
Perfumes, yes, but cooking, no.

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:59.440
We drink frankincense water.

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:03.160
'And after some persuasion, I managed to get a chance to capture my own frankincense.'

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:08.760
Literally, the sap appears instantly and within seconds of taking this first layer off.

00:11:10.120 --> 00:11:13.240
So, can I taste the sap from this one?

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:14.040
Can I have a little taste?

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:15.200
Yeah, OK.

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:16.480
You can taste it.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:23.400
It's bitter, eh?

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:23.800
Really bitter!

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:25.440
Exactly.

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:25.720
Yeah.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:29.600
It was bitter, but at the same time, it was really complex.

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:35.160
It had a wonderful aromatic... fragrance to it.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:37.200
A fruity note as well.

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:44.040
But more than anything else, I just found there was something...mystical about it.

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:50.800
I know what frankincense tastes like, but from being there, from that tree...

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:53.720
It gave me something I couldn't put your finger on...

00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:54.720
Otherworldly.

00:11:54.560 --> 00:12:02.840
Before I carried on in my search to find the next of my three gifts, there was time to share a special local drink - fresh camel's milk.

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:04.000
Camel milk.

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:16.200
OK, here.

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:23.200
It's good milk.

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:23.720
Good?

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:24.280
It's very good.

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:26.360
Now to the meal.

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:32.160
I decided to create a frankincense tea to include in my dish.

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:37.080
So this magical piece of kit is essentially a distillator.

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:43.840
Here, I have the frankincense sap, crystallised, that I brought back from Oman.

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:50.680
And the idea of this is to actually make an extraction - at a low temperature - of frankincense.

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:56.320
'This machine creates a vacuum which causes water to boil at 40 degrees.

00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:58.560
'The vapour is then condensed to create a frankincense hydrosol.

00:12:58.280 --> 00:13:03.360
'Extracting at low temperature means that much more of those magical qualities will be preserved.'

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:08.240
So this is my frankincense water.

00:13:10.120 --> 00:13:15.440
With frankincense captured, next on my quest was myrrh.

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:19.160
For this, I headed to see some local healers who regularly use it as medicine.

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:24.480
When I arrived at their shop, I found it to be a treasure trove of exciting concoctions.

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:31.280
They call it the hand of mother Jesus, this one.

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:31.360
Is it?

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:37.160
It's like Viagra.

00:13:38.680 --> 00:13:40.440
This is the best.

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:43.280
Class-A myrrh?

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:43.640
Yes.

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:48.640
You're looking at it and you think it looks a bit like a stone, but when you touch it and it's not, you can see it's crystallised sap.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:57.640
'The purest myrrh comes straight from the trunk of a tree 'and is one of the most important local medicinal ingredients.

00:13:56.880 --> 00:14:00.760
'It's used in all manner of ways, one of which is burning it to help sore throats.'

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:10.720
'It might be good for a cold, but it wasn't something I wanted to give my guests.

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:15.440
'The next option was myrrh mixed with honey.'

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:20.400
It's always better to eat after 24 hours.

00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:24.520
'The honey mix was still too bitter to use in my meal.

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:26.920
'It tasted very much like rubber.

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:30.400
'How about eating myrrh in its rawest form?'

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:35.680
What would happen if I was just to bite a piece of myrrh?

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:54.160
It's quite bitter, but... that's only because I've just eaten it raw.

00:14:55.560 --> 00:15:11.880
I'm thinking how on earth am I going to get this into the dish, because... unlike the frankincense, which was wonderfully aromatic and quite hedonistic... ..there was an aniseed-like quality about the myrrh, but there was also a real bitterness and a slight rubbery note to it.

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:13.760
That was going to be a real challenge.

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:22.120
'Back in the lab, I had to try and solve the problem of working the very medicinal myrrh into the dish.

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:28.160
'My hope was that in the time since we were in Oman, the myrrh oil and honey it had been mixed with 'might have mellowed its bitterness.'

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:32.960
Oh, God!

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:34.000
Oh, God!

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:35.720
That is so bitter.

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:43.360
I wouldn't want that as a birthday present!

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:50.360
Getting the myrrh in is going to be a bit of a job.

00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:53.760
'I still wanted all three of the Wise Men's gifts in my dish.

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:57.360
'I wasn't about to give up on myrrh yet.

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:02.560
'So I headed back to the source of the ingredient - the tree it comes from.'

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:12.680
I've got a feeling it's not, but there's only one way to find out.

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:15.920
I'm trying to make myrrh tea.

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:24.280
Just as I thought.

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:25.840
Absolutely useless!

00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:34.360
Everything we did with it, it was bitter.

00:16:33.520 --> 00:16:41.040
And then we found ourselves trying to work out how we can put myrrh in the dish and then disguise it.

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:42.360
And I thought, "Actually, what on earth's the point of doing this?"

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:44.520
It shouldn't be in there.

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:52.920
To make the dish delicious, we had to leave the myrrh out and bring it into the starter in a different form.

00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:53.640
So I've come up with this.

00:16:53.880 --> 00:17:00.280
It's a teaspoon, whittled from what we had left of the myrrh branch I brought back from Oman.

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:12.800
One of the key moments of any Nativity play is when the Wise Men bring their gifts to the newborn baby.

00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:22.319
To give my guests a clue to the first course, hidden in their crackers, I gave them a small wafer that, when eaten, tasted just like the smell of a baby.

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:23.920
"A babe in a manger.

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:25.599
"Put it into your mouth and it melts magically."

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:27.520
Yeah.

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:27.960
And let it dissolve.

00:17:27.200 --> 00:17:28.520
Under the tongue.

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:36.760
It's gone.

00:17:37.320 --> 00:17:38.600
Mm!

00:17:37.640 --> 00:17:39.120
I'm not going to call on your mother!

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:40.440
What is it?

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:45.120
It's Johnson's Baby Powder.

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:45.920
It's Johnson's talc.

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:50.440
While my guests puzzled, I needed to add the final element to my Three Wise Men dish - gold.

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:56.720
And my inspiration for that came from a visit to the Muscat marketplace in Oman.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:06.200
The idea is that this is the third part of my triangle, I suppose - frankincense, myrrh and gold.

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:17.720
Obviously, as a chef, gold is not uncommon to use, but I just thought to be able to get it from the same place as, hopefully, I'll be getting frankincense and myrrh, it'll have that historical element and just make it more special.

00:18:28.880 --> 00:18:32.000
Hand-hammered 21-carat gold, ready for use.

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:36.400
For making jewellery, that is.

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:46.600
Unfortunately, it's about 100 times too thick for me.

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:49.320
And the other slight complication... it's not edible.

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:57.480
Back in the lab, I needed to find a way to get gold into the dish.

00:18:57.640 --> 00:19:04.440
This is 23-carat gold leaf for decorating food and beverages.

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:06.520
Gold fingers!

00:19:11.480 --> 00:19:14.360
It's completely edible... and it's completely and utterly tasteless.

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:24.120
My idea was to use the gold leaf to wrap a stock cube and make a gold ingot, but to do that I first needed to make the stock.

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:35.720
This is a langoustine broth which has been reinforced with quite a large amount of onions, and cooked off in star anise, with a reduction of vermouth and other vegetables, aromatics and herbs.

00:19:34.760 --> 00:19:43.760
And the reason for freezing it is because this next method, which is ice filtration, is just the most wonderful way to clarify a stock.

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:49.040
The frozen stock is wrapped in muslin and left for 24 hours.

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:59.280
What then happens is that the gelatine in the stock acts as a filter and as the stock thaws it holds behind its solids, giving an amazingly clear stock.

00:19:58.520 --> 00:20:01.960
This is a great technique that can be used with all stocks containing gelatine.

00:20:02.440 --> 00:20:06.840
The next stage for this is to freeze it in a minus-80 freezer.

00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:15.560
Once the stock is frozen, I then move it to another piece of industrial equipment I own - a freeze drier - to remove all the moisture.

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:18.320
This needs to go in here for about a day-and-a-half.

00:20:21.240 --> 00:20:24.600
This is the langoustine stock that has come out of the freeze drier.

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:30.840
You can see that it's taken on this amazing kind of honeycomb effect.

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:33.200
You've got to work very quickly because this will suck up moisture from the air.

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:41.640
I then need to push the dry stock into my gold bar mould.

00:20:40.760 --> 00:20:42.040
So there it is.

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:46.280
That's my langoustine stock cube.

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:48.440
The next stage - I need to cover it in gold leaf.

00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:55.080
And there is my gold bullion bar.

00:20:54.120 --> 00:21:00.240
With all the gifts of the Three Wise Men finally included, it was time to turn my attention to the garnish for the dish.

00:21:00.040 --> 00:21:04.520
I add egg yolk cooked with saffron to the plate.

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:08.080
Langoustine meat from the shells we used for the stock cube.

00:21:07.160 --> 00:21:13.920
Next, a coffee and saffron bavarois.

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:17.000
Pistachios, lightly crystallised...

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:23.560
And tiny onions, lightly cooked and slightly pickled.

00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:31.240
Then, to add a real perfume to the dish, sea urchin.

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:35.320
They have a wonderful violet character, which will work really well with the frankincense.

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:38.000
Finally, some tarragon tips and chervil leaf.

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:42.040
Not chopped, because that will destroy its delicate characteristic.

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:53.560
So, here's my first course - gold...frankincense...and myrrh.

00:21:58.560 --> 00:22:01.880
So, take the gold ingot...

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:03.120
Ooh, smell the tea!

00:22:02.160 --> 00:22:04.080
Place the gold ingot in the glass teapot.

00:22:07.640 --> 00:22:09.400
Pour the frankincense tea over the ingot.

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:10.280
Stir.

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:17.400
It looks like a bouillon with pretensions.

00:22:16.520 --> 00:22:18.040
Look at that.

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:18.640
That's lovely.

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:20.280
The leaf is coming off.

00:22:19.760 --> 00:22:21.200
Stir.

00:22:20.440 --> 00:22:26.760
I loved the luxury of the fact that out came this ingot of gold leaf, and then you poured a little sort of tea over it.

00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:34.720
And the gold leaf floated into a million little pieces.

00:22:35.720 --> 00:22:39.000
These were made in Oman.

00:22:42.560 --> 00:22:48.560
I'm eating gold... and frankincense and myrrh.

00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:48.920
Mm!

00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:50.040
The taste of the broth is amazing.

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:56.360
And it's enhanced by the fact that we're eating gold leaf along with it.

00:22:55.440 --> 00:23:00.400
It has very, very delicate and intense flavours.

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:02.400
It looks so pretty as well.

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:06.480
I hope it's not going to make our Christmas dinner at home look a bit shabby!

00:23:08.560 --> 00:23:14.480
It's completely insanely, bonkersly, madly, Englishly...fantastic.

00:23:13.560 --> 00:23:16.200
There are so many different tastes.

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:21.880
You can't make it at home, so you know that there's one person on the planet who is making it, and it's this mad inventor.

00:23:23.240 --> 00:23:28.760
I tell you what, if I'd been served this when I was born, I'd have started a religion.

00:23:34.960 --> 00:23:55.200
I had an uncle I was really fond of, and we used to go round to his house every Christmas and he had this wonderful red leather armchair and there was always a fire and he would smoke a pipe and have a glass of malt.

00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:58.440
And it was just so Christmassy to me.

00:23:57.720 --> 00:24:02.320
I wanted to try and somehow encapsulate all of that into this next course.

00:24:03.520 --> 00:24:13.640
I had to try and get those amazing smells of whisky, leather and wood into my next dish, which I decided would be a palate-cleansing sorbet.

00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:18.400
This is a leather extract.

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:26.080
You have to be careful with this one.

00:24:25.240 --> 00:24:27.360
If it's too high, it's just like chewing a leather handbag.

00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:27.920
Mm.

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:29.320
Not bad, that.

00:24:29.840 --> 00:24:32.880
This is pipe tobacco.

00:24:34.200 --> 00:24:39.680
MACHINE WHIRRS Can you see it?

00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:47.760
So, it's sorbet...delicately licked...by pipe tobacco smoke.

00:24:53.440 --> 00:25:00.600
'OK, so simply blowing smoke on to the sorbet wasn't going to work, but I wasn't giving up.'

00:25:01.440 --> 00:25:02.640
We need to smell them.

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:03.880
I need it to be in the air.

00:25:03.040 --> 00:25:11.280
I need to find a way of bringing the smell into the... serving of this dish, in such a way that it will really conjure up that memory.

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:24.680
What I needed was an expert in smell to help we capture those nostalgic aromas, and for that, I went to Paris and IFF - a leading perfumery where they create some of the world's best-known scents.

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:30.080
I'd come to meet Christophe Laudamiel, a kindred spirit of mine who is a genius with smell.

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:41.520
For Christmas, people have a lot of things with spices, cinnamon and orange, then mulberry, and cloves and laurel.

00:25:40.680 --> 00:25:43.960
Cookies, gingerbread....

00:25:43.120 --> 00:25:49.880
At Christmas time, it's freezing cold and there will be somebody there with chestnuts roasting.

00:25:49.360 --> 00:25:52.040
And I always loved the smell.

00:25:51.160 --> 00:25:56.800
For all of us, smell at Christmas time is hugely evocative.

00:25:55.840 --> 00:26:01.000
The challenge for me was being able to create those natural smells in a magical way.

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:05.800
How many aroma molecules have you to work with?

00:26:04.920 --> 00:26:07.720
Well, molecules, I would say around 1,000.

00:26:07.800 --> 00:26:13.320
Very often you have 20, 50, 100.

00:26:12.360 --> 00:26:17.040
This is very common in France - a formula with 50 ingredients, 100 ingredients, and people can't believe this.

00:26:16.280 --> 00:26:20.320
They think we mix three or four things together.

00:26:19.520 --> 00:26:26.920
Even nature is not like that - our own smell is 300 molecules.

00:26:26.040 --> 00:26:30.160
So, having inspired Christophe, he set to work creating my smells.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:39.560
He used all manner of techniques from an electric sniffing device that breaks down fragrances, to computer programmes that analyse every molecule.

00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:44.720
But despite all the technology, the best instrument we had was still the nose.

00:26:44.920 --> 00:26:48.120
So, the tobacco is stronger...

00:26:47.320 --> 00:26:50.200
Yes, the leather is coming through.

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:50.400
And the leather comes through also.

00:26:51.440 --> 00:26:56.240
This is a really nice balance.

00:26:57.240 --> 00:27:03.760
Here, we are trying to recreate this nice tobacco feeling, and maybe a certain woody-ness.

00:27:03.320 --> 00:27:07.680
The smell is a three-dimensional thing.

00:27:06.720 --> 00:27:09.560
You breathe it, it surrounds you.

00:27:10.080 --> 00:27:15.080
The hard-wiring that goes on with smell taps into memory and you can't control it.

00:27:15.680 --> 00:27:20.720
You've just about got the whole smell of that wonderful old room.

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:24.600
The only thing I haven't got is the fire.

00:27:23.760 --> 00:27:30.680
My idea was to get the fireplace into the meal by setting fire to the sorbet.

00:27:29.760 --> 00:27:35.760
To do this, I'm going to add a special heat-resistant substance called gellan.

00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:38.040
This will allow the sorbet to flame without melting.

00:27:37.880 --> 00:27:40.720
To begin with, I add water to a pan.

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:45.440
Then to that, I add freeze-dried apple for a deep, appley flavour.

00:27:45.720 --> 00:27:51.240
Apple juice, and this - malic acid.

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:57.680
Malic acid is the acid that is predominant in apples and it gives a really pleasing... sensation on the side of the tongue.

00:27:56.800 --> 00:28:00.040
It generates saliva, which makes food mouth-watering.

00:28:00.440 --> 00:28:04.520
And finally the magic ingredient - the gellan.

00:28:04.560 --> 00:28:08.360
We just need to bring this to the boil, mixing while we do so.

00:28:11.480 --> 00:28:18.000
I have to mix this as I heat it, to stop the gellan forming into a gel.

00:28:21.880 --> 00:28:24.480
I then need to cool that down really quickly.

00:28:26.600 --> 00:28:28.400
I'll just pop these containers into an ice bath.

00:28:31.520 --> 00:28:37.760
After cooling, the mix is then churned in a sorbet machine.

00:28:36.920 --> 00:28:43.800
I've churned this, but as with all sorbets and ice creams, it's very important to make sure you've got as small ice crystals as possible.

00:28:42.960 --> 00:28:51.440
If they're too large, you'll detect them in the mouth and between the teeth and the ice cream will feel icy and not be as smooth as it should be.

00:28:55.080 --> 00:28:57.000
Here I've got some whisky.

00:28:58.120 --> 00:28:59.600
Time to check the mix.

00:29:03.640 --> 00:29:05.680
So, there we are.

00:29:04.880 --> 00:29:09.080
A flaming sorbet that really doesn't melt!

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:13.600
For serving this sorbet, first, take the bowl.

00:29:12.800 --> 00:29:15.160
I've had this covered with leather.

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:19.840
The idea is when you're eating it, the touch is going to help the memory and the smell that we're going to be presenting with this dish.

00:29:24.800 --> 00:29:29.200
Into that, I'm going to put the hot cast-iron casserole dish.

00:29:28.320 --> 00:29:30.840
And this is what the sorbet is actually going to sit in.

00:29:34.480 --> 00:29:39.760
Here I've got some cubes of apple that have just been macerated in an acidulated syrup.

00:29:40.760 --> 00:29:44.200
And the sorbet will sit on top of the cubes.

00:29:43.320 --> 00:29:44.800
So, here's the sorbet going into the pot.

00:29:49.360 --> 00:29:54.640
It's now ready to go to my guests, so they can do the fun bit and set it on fire.

00:29:58.960 --> 00:30:01.000
This is a leather plate.

00:30:00.120 --> 00:30:02.400
A leather plate?!

00:30:01.800 --> 00:30:03.240
Aha!

00:30:08.960 --> 00:30:12.000
Now, you pour the whisky over.

00:30:24.040 --> 00:30:30.080
You pour the Jack Daniels on to the sorbet - that went against all my instincts.

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:32.680
You light it...

00:30:34.720 --> 00:30:36.440
And then you start tasting.

00:30:36.200 --> 00:30:38.240
Delicious!

00:30:38.440 --> 00:30:46.880
So, we've got our flaming sorbet - there's our fireplace - but where is the smell of the leather and where's the smell of the fire?

00:30:46.160 --> 00:30:48.160
It's in the roses.

00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:53.240
It's in the flower display on the table.

00:30:53.720 --> 00:30:59.560
It was underneath the roses, underneath the vase - in the bigger container that it sat in, we put a bed of crushed dry ice.

00:30:58.680 --> 00:31:01.640
Dry ice is wonderful at extracting aroma.

00:31:00.880 --> 00:31:12.680
So Eric arrived at the table with a teapot that was full of hot water with the aroma that Christophe made - the smell of the room.

00:31:11.800 --> 00:31:15.520
Wow!

00:31:19.320 --> 00:31:20.480
That is fantastic.

00:31:19.560 --> 00:31:24.440
Now, I can't see my spoon.

00:31:26.480 --> 00:31:30.480
And you've got that intense wood smoke which always catches in your clothes.

00:31:29.600 --> 00:31:35.880
What was lovely is you were sitting at the table and you could smell it.

00:31:35.080 --> 00:31:36.480
Wow!

00:31:42.800 --> 00:31:44.960
Ain't this wonderful?

00:31:44.120 --> 00:31:50.440
The leather is warm, the cocotte is warm, and the sorbet is still freezing.

00:31:54.440 --> 00:31:55.520
Yeah.

00:31:54.640 --> 00:31:57.120
THEY LAUGH Wow!

00:31:57.280 --> 00:32:00.920
It was fun.

00:32:00.320 --> 00:32:02.120
That's the whole point about it.

00:32:01.400 --> 00:32:07.880
Yes, the flavours were extraordinary and different and a combination the like of which you're not going to get anywhere else, but it was also fun.

00:32:08.480 --> 00:32:10.080
Great fun.

00:32:09.160 --> 00:32:13.160
I mean, you're setting fire to a sorbet!

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:14.480
Don't be ridiculous!

00:32:20.360 --> 00:32:23.080
I knew I was going to use goose for the main course.

00:32:22.120 --> 00:32:25.360
That's what I've had for the last ten years at Christmas.

00:32:24.560 --> 00:32:27.840
But where were we going to get the goose from?

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:42.200
Goose, to me, is all about history, conjuring up images of Christmas past, so my first stop to find a goose was Hampton Court Palace, where they've been roasting the geese of kings and queens for centuries.

00:32:41.400 --> 00:32:48.760
To understand more about this grand old bird, I met up with Marc Meltonville, who runs the Palace kitchens.

00:32:50.840 --> 00:32:55.640
What is the history of roast goose in Britain?

00:32:54.880 --> 00:32:59.200
As far as I can tell, it goes back so far we don't actually know.

00:32:58.680 --> 00:33:04.280
It's one of those dishes linked with Christmas, but it goes back long before Christmas.

00:33:03.360 --> 00:33:13.400
It seems that we ate geese at the harvest festival in pre-Roman times, and I couldn't work out why till I started reading into it.

00:33:12.840 --> 00:33:18.840
Geese, along with leaves, fruits and everything, they disappear.

00:33:18.080 --> 00:33:21.520
So the leaves fall off the trees, the light goes and the geese depart.

00:33:20.760 --> 00:33:28.000
We know they migrate, but THEY didn't know where they went, and so they became part of the festival of death and rebirth.

00:33:27.360 --> 00:33:29.200
This is ready now, is it?

00:33:47.480 --> 00:33:52.640
I don't see why more people don't have this, instead of turkey, for Christmas.

00:33:51.680 --> 00:33:53.160
No.

00:33:52.280 --> 00:33:58.040
What's got me instantly is the amount of juice, not fat, but the amount of juice that's still in there.

00:33:57.240 --> 00:33:58.560
It's nice.

00:33:57.600 --> 00:34:00.040
It's delicious.

00:34:00.800 --> 00:34:09.320
The long Christmas tradition and amazing taste that comes with goose was clear, but I was no closer to finding one for my meal.

00:34:09.400 --> 00:34:17.040
We sourced four geese, all reared in different environments and on different feeds, in order to find my perfect bird.

00:34:18.159 --> 00:34:23.120
You get that... like the fat from a grain-fed bird.

00:34:22.320 --> 00:34:26.280
There's something fruity, almost, there.

00:34:25.400 --> 00:34:27.960
For me, it's between these two.

00:34:27.840 --> 00:34:36.120
One of the things that could be exciting is that if we feed the goose on a particular food, can that be tasted in the end result?

00:34:40.639 --> 00:34:48.320
For me, the tastiest bird came from Essex and the farm of Simon Hughes, so that's where I headed.

00:34:47.719 --> 00:34:54.560
Simon's been rearing birds on a diet of wheat and grass for years, and has his own unique way of calling the birds to dinner.

00:34:53.679 --> 00:34:54.920
Come on, the geese!

00:34:56.679 --> 00:34:58.080
Hey-hey-hey!

00:35:03.160 --> 00:35:06.520
So they get fed now?

00:35:05.800 --> 00:35:09.400
You've got the feed tubs here - what's in there?

00:35:09.280 --> 00:35:12.800
That will be just wheat in those.

00:35:12.000 --> 00:35:14.240
Are they feeding off the land here?

00:35:14.240 --> 00:35:16.680
Yeah, they're eating the grass.

00:35:15.760 --> 00:35:18.920
They eat a lot of stones and things out of the ground.

00:35:18.080 --> 00:35:21.320
You see that it's full of holes.

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:21.520
I'm just looking at them eating that trailer!

00:35:23.640 --> 00:35:26.680
"Oh, yes, I'll have a nice bit of undercarriage!"

00:35:28.800 --> 00:35:34.960
I'd established that geese will eat almost everything, which is perfect for my plan.

00:35:34.280 --> 00:35:38.920
The way meat tastes can be affected by the way in which the animal is fed.

00:35:38.160 --> 00:35:41.520
I had an idea.

00:35:40.560 --> 00:35:49.880
I was going to feed my geese the essence of Douglas fir pine, which I hoped would make them taste a little bit like Christmas tree.

00:35:49.040 --> 00:35:50.360
Yeah.

00:35:51.600 --> 00:35:52.760
They're gathering round, aren't they?

00:35:51.880 --> 00:35:56.600
That's definitely the action when they eat a new food.

00:35:55.640 --> 00:35:57.360
Oh, I think we're on to a winner there.

00:35:58.040 --> 00:35:59.840
A success.

00:36:01.160 --> 00:36:02.360
I think it's a definite success.

00:36:01.520 --> 00:36:10.360
I'd found that the geese would eat the pine essence, which meant that I could control the way they tasted.

00:36:09.720 --> 00:36:11.240
The next vital factor was catching myself a goose.

00:36:11.120 --> 00:36:14.520
So what is the technique for picking these up, then?

00:36:24.440 --> 00:36:25.960
So we're going to house them somewhere else.

00:36:32.640 --> 00:36:41.920
We've made a little grotto.

00:36:41.320 --> 00:36:46.240
'Animals that live in a peaceful and stress-free environment really do taste better.

00:36:45.480 --> 00:36:53.040
'For the next two months, I wanted my geese to have the best life they could, 'so I built them their own special Christmas home.'

00:36:55.200 --> 00:36:56.960
Oh, no!

00:37:00.200 --> 00:37:04.160
Who built that thing?

00:37:06.320 --> 00:37:08.920
How is that going to keep a goose in?

00:37:14.560 --> 00:37:20.960
After a bit of a struggle, I finally got my geese into their new home.

00:37:33.360 --> 00:37:41.840
'With the geese happy, I was off to make them some special feed 'that I hoped would make them taste really Christmassy.'

00:37:41.480 --> 00:37:46.600
The idea is we've actually got the poultry feed.

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:58.200
That's 1.6kg and we want to try and get it mixed and stuck to these pine flakes, so we'll get a Christmas-tree flavour.

00:37:58.680 --> 00:38:00.880
And some freeze-dried apple.

00:38:01.400 --> 00:38:04.040
We've got some lucky geese here!

00:38:03.200 --> 00:38:09.320
We need to eat this, because if it's good enough for us, it's good enough for a goose.

00:38:08.480 --> 00:38:10.240
If it's not good enough for us, I ain't giving it to the geese.

00:38:12.760 --> 00:38:22.160
'To the feed ingredients I also added the syrup, which, when heated, 'would hold it all together and make the first-ever three-Michelin-star goose feed.'

00:38:30.560 --> 00:38:32.200
It's not too bad, is it?

00:38:31.280 --> 00:38:32.800
Yes.

00:38:31.920 --> 00:38:39.600
You get the pine, the apple comes through and the sage and onion definitely comes through.

00:38:38.760 --> 00:38:42.560
In the background you get the wheaty note from the feed.

00:38:41.720 --> 00:38:48.360
If my geese eat that mixture, they should then taste of apple and pine.

00:38:47.520 --> 00:38:50.920
The one thing I hadn't realised was just how much they'd actually eat.

00:38:50.240 --> 00:38:53.560
Almost 20 bags of feed.

00:38:52.760 --> 00:38:57.200
To make all that, we needed to be working on a bigger scale.

00:39:15.600 --> 00:39:22.920
So, here it is, our Christmas goose, fattened on a mixture of wheat and Christmas tree.

00:39:22.080 --> 00:39:24.840
The idea is to use every part of the goose in the meal.

00:39:24.040 --> 00:39:26.800
Nothing will go to waste.

00:39:27.200 --> 00:39:32.440
The legs I take off, to cook in the traditional way, which I'll come to later.

00:39:31.480 --> 00:39:37.640
Then the skin is removed, which I'll roast separately to get an amazing crispness.

00:39:36.840 --> 00:39:38.920
Then the breast.

00:39:38.120 --> 00:39:46.520
I'll vacuum-pack them in goose fat and cook them in a 56-degree water bath, which maintains all the moisture and flavour in the meat.

00:39:45.600 --> 00:39:48.120
Now, back to the goose legs.

00:39:48.840 --> 00:39:54.480
This recipe is called powdered goose and was popular in the 17th century.

00:39:53.560 --> 00:40:05.160
I've cooked the goose legs very gently in goose fat for 50 hours at 65 degrees, and then smoked them for 20 minutes over cherry chips, giving them a delicate smoky flavour.

00:40:08.160 --> 00:40:13.640
These will then be shredded and added to the veloute that I'm going to serve with the goose.

00:40:13.800 --> 00:40:17.640
Now to my chestnut veloute.

00:40:16.720 --> 00:40:23.080
I've brushed a bowl with hazelnut butter and coated it with ground hazelnuts.

00:40:22.440 --> 00:40:26.200
And here I'm piping in some pureed potatoes... to the bottom of the bowl.

00:40:31.520 --> 00:40:34.320
On top of that, I'm going to add some of the powdered goose.

00:40:33.480 --> 00:40:38.880
These are the confit-ed goose legs that we smoked over cherry chips.

00:40:41.240 --> 00:40:49.200
On top of that, I'm going to add...some pickled chestnuts ..and some white truffle.

00:40:49.480 --> 00:40:57.760
Then finally, the chestnut veloute, a wonderfully rich mix made with chestnuts, butter and the stock from the goose.

00:40:59.560 --> 00:41:03.880
To top it, I'll just put the cover on with some chestnuts on.

00:41:04.280 --> 00:41:07.600
And it's missing the key thing.

00:41:06.680 --> 00:41:11.800
This is based on chestnuts roasting on an open fire, so I need chestnuts roasted on an open fire.

00:41:13.240 --> 00:41:22.120
I take a glass cloche and place it over some roasting chestnuts, into which the smoke then rises.

00:41:21.160 --> 00:41:30.920
The idea of this is the guests, when they first take the soup, they pull the jar off and turn it upside down, then they'll get the full impact of the smell of chestnuts roasting on an open fire.

00:41:33.440 --> 00:41:36.320
So, now to finish my goose main course.

00:41:39.440 --> 00:41:43.160
First off, in here I've got some fresh black pudding.

00:41:42.320 --> 00:41:51.280
This is cooked on a low temperature for a long time, continually mixing to get this wonderful, rich, velvety chocolate consistency.

00:41:50.440 --> 00:42:01.680
I then carve the breasts into thick pieces, which I lightly glaze with a sauce made from wine, the goose bones and lots of spices.

00:42:00.840 --> 00:42:04.880
Then I add some grelot onions.

00:42:08.040 --> 00:42:15.560
Next, some small turnips cooked in a butter and water mixture.

00:42:15.920 --> 00:42:21.680
Here is goose skin that we've... roasted and lacquered.

00:42:22.160 --> 00:42:27.680
Now some salt that I've flavoured with the same pine that I fed to the geese.

00:42:30.880 --> 00:42:38.760
Some sauce... a few drops of sage oil to mirror the stuffing we fed to the geese... then some pickling brine.

00:42:39.120 --> 00:42:44.280
And finally, the goose cracker.

00:42:43.360 --> 00:42:47.000
And there is my perfect Christmas goose.

00:43:04.320 --> 00:43:06.160
Oh, yeah, look at that!

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:12.640
It brings me back it our house, the old Christmases around the electric fire.

00:43:11.680 --> 00:43:12.880
(Wow!)

00:43:12.040 --> 00:43:13.920
Don't eat the chestnuts.

00:43:16.720 --> 00:43:18.360
The veloute is fantastic.

00:43:17.440 --> 00:43:18.680
Oh, look at the veloute.

00:43:17.720 --> 00:43:18.920
The veloute's amazing.

00:43:26.360 --> 00:43:29.400
And it just became this fabulous taste.

00:43:28.520 --> 00:43:31.400
There wasn't a word off that table.

00:43:33.080 --> 00:43:39.160
There was just complete silence and six inveterate ego maniacs and "look at me, look at me" people just can't talk because they're eating!

00:43:38.320 --> 00:43:39.840
Oh, that's lovely!

00:43:44.760 --> 00:43:46.680
Oh, that is fantastic.

00:43:45.800 --> 00:43:46.920
That is really, really up there for me.

00:43:46.080 --> 00:43:47.880
It's one of the nicest things I've ever had.

00:43:50.680 --> 00:43:54.120
God, it was really...

00:43:53.280 --> 00:43:57.160
That is the sort of food that I'm going to go home and cry about in an hour's time.

00:43:56.200 --> 00:43:57.760
That's lovely, isn't it?

00:43:56.840 --> 00:43:58.080
That is gorgeous.

00:43:57.200 --> 00:43:58.640
I absolutely love it.

00:44:04.160 --> 00:44:05.240
This is just delicious.

00:44:04.360 --> 00:44:05.840
The flavour...

00:44:04.920 --> 00:44:07.120
It's just the intensity.

00:44:07.440 --> 00:44:11.200
I've eaten in some of the best Little Chefs in the world, but this...

00:44:10.400 --> 00:44:12.560
You've eaten some of the best little chefs?

00:44:12.600 --> 00:44:14.240
Yes, but this is a step up.

00:44:13.400 --> 00:44:14.560
I'll be honest.

00:44:18.920 --> 00:44:26.840
It was - Sue felt the same - it's something I'll remember until I'm in my box.

00:44:26.000 --> 00:44:28.720
Probably never to be repeated.

00:44:36.880 --> 00:44:41.400
So far, we've got quite a few Christmas memories in this dinner.

00:44:40.720 --> 00:44:48.200
But there's a key one that we're missing - Rudolph the red-nosed reindeer.

00:44:47.280 --> 00:44:51.800
And to find Rudolph, we went to Siberia.

00:44:58.600 --> 00:45:07.760
I braved minus-30 degree temperatures to visit northern Russia, because this is a place where reindeer are an integral part of the lives of the local people.

00:45:07.040 --> 00:45:10.240
I arrived at a time of celebration.

00:45:09.360 --> 00:45:17.280
It was the beginning of the reindeer season, a time of plenty for the local Evenk tribe which they mark by holding reindeer festivals.

00:45:28.400 --> 00:45:35.280
Then, with my welcome over, it was time for the best bit of any festival, the eating and drinking.

00:45:35.200 --> 00:45:38.400
Russian vodka.

00:45:41.200 --> 00:45:44.560
Ruskan vodka.

00:45:52.800 --> 00:45:58.680
THEY TOAST IN EVENKI 'Then, I had my first taste of Siberian reindeer.'

00:45:57.720 --> 00:46:03.600
It is marrow bone, so it is the marrow from inside the bone.

00:46:13.240 --> 00:46:14.320
It's delicious.

00:46:16.520 --> 00:46:17.600
It's very rich.

00:46:17.320 --> 00:46:19.560
Rich and fatty.

00:46:23.720 --> 00:46:27.800
Next on the menu, reindeer kidney.

00:46:36.480 --> 00:46:40.160
It's like an aphrodisiac.

00:46:50.000 --> 00:46:53.600
I don't care if that's an aphrodisiac or not.

00:46:52.760 --> 00:46:55.560
It tastes fantastic.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:07.000
If it's an aphrodisiac, I don't want to be in here in a minute - she's eaten a whole raw kidney!

00:47:10.240 --> 00:47:15.920
Needing to cool down a little, I headed back into the snow and found yet more reindeer concoctions.

00:47:15.000 --> 00:47:16.120
Come and look over here.

00:47:15.240 --> 00:47:16.560
Come.

00:47:29.960 --> 00:47:31.280
Reindeer tea.

00:47:30.440 --> 00:47:34.320
It's a kind of refined, upmarket Cup-A-Soup.

00:47:33.520 --> 00:47:38.560
Then, with a skinful of vodka and a belly full of reindeer, it was time for a bit of a dance.

00:47:44.920 --> 00:47:47.920
But I still had a worry.

00:47:46.960 --> 00:47:54.520
These people live with reindeer, so they eat the reindeer, they wear the reindeer, the reindeer is their transport.

00:47:53.800 --> 00:47:55.800
They love their animals.

00:47:54.960 --> 00:47:56.160
They love the reindeer.

00:47:55.200 --> 00:47:59.640
Their whole lives are intertwined with reindeer.

00:47:58.880 --> 00:48:03.680
Well, that's not the case in Britain.

00:48:02.760 --> 00:48:07.120
And I'm sorry, but I just couldn't bring myself to cook Santa's little helper.

00:48:06.480 --> 00:48:08.080
It just didn't seem right.

00:48:16.120 --> 00:48:22.240
I had to come up with another solution, so I headed to a reindeer farm.

00:48:34.520 --> 00:48:43.960
On this farm, the reindeer are raised like cattle, but with temperatures reaching minus-60 at times, they're a little tougher than the average Friesian.

00:48:43.120 --> 00:48:47.560
This particular farm had over 1,000 reindeer in their herd.

00:48:47.920 --> 00:48:50.440
This is amazing.

00:48:49.480 --> 00:48:51.680
We spent five hours getting here.

00:48:51.160 --> 00:48:53.360
Talk about off the beaten track.

00:48:52.440 --> 00:49:00.280
It was three hours on one road and, um... seeing this, it's all completely worth it.

00:49:06.960 --> 00:49:11.520
Now the interesting thing I've learnt is the reindeers with the big antlers are the females.

00:49:12.360 --> 00:49:13.480
The males are the ones predominantly without antlers.

00:49:19.600 --> 00:49:23.400
The reindeer is completely intertwined with people's lives here.

00:49:22.480 --> 00:49:27.480
It's what they live off.

00:49:27.960 --> 00:49:33.360
I can't kill Santa's little helper for my Christmas lunch, but I do have an idea.

00:49:35.840 --> 00:49:37.920
Reindeer milk!

00:49:46.880 --> 00:49:49.840
MAN HOLDING REINDEER WHISTLES I can't see the teat.

00:49:48.880 --> 00:49:50.560
There.

00:50:00.400 --> 00:50:03.320
Well, I'm milking myself a reindeer!

00:50:02.480 --> 00:50:05.680
I never thought I'd see the day.

00:50:18.960 --> 00:50:22.160
It's so cold here, it's frozen, like the...

00:50:21.200 --> 00:50:24.480
Perhaps I should pull the hair out first!

00:50:34.840 --> 00:50:36.160
Mmm(!)

00:50:35.240 --> 00:50:37.520
It's incredibly rich.

00:50:36.800 --> 00:50:39.080
It's kind of...

00:50:38.240 --> 00:50:41.840
It's just a really creamy cow's milk.

00:50:41.840 --> 00:50:47.640
Apparently, frozen reindeer milk is quite a big thing around here and I can see why - it tastes delicious.

00:50:46.840 --> 00:50:50.400
This might have just given me an idea.

00:50:52.800 --> 00:50:55.480
I've got the milk.

00:50:54.640 --> 00:50:56.720
Now all I've got to do is get home.

00:51:02.440 --> 00:51:15.480
For me, at Christmas, the most exciting time of the day is breakfast, so...the idea is to use the reindeer milk and turn it into breakfast.

00:51:14.720 --> 00:51:18.160
We're basing it on scrambled eggs on toast.

00:51:17.320 --> 00:51:23.360
For the toast, I'm actually going to make some brioches, which is, essentially, really buttery bread.

00:51:22.400 --> 00:51:27.680
But the difference with these is I've incorporated some mincemeat into the dough.

00:51:26.760 --> 00:51:30.120
These need to go into the oven for about 45 minutes.

00:51:29.160 --> 00:51:32.000
Now, to my reindeer milk.

00:51:31.200 --> 00:51:39.680
I've decided to turn it into ice cream because of how great the frozen milk tasted in Siberia and to put it on top of my brioches.

00:51:40.360 --> 00:51:45.880
To make this, I start by mixing egg yolks and unrefined castor sugar.

00:51:47.200 --> 00:51:50.440
This has a slight molasses note, which adds a lovely complexity to the dish.

00:51:52.960 --> 00:51:58.080
The eggs and sugar have been whisking for about five minutes, and now I need to heat some milk up.

00:51:58.720 --> 00:52:00.640
Here I've got the reindeer milk.

00:51:59.680 --> 00:52:10.760
This is very high fat, between 16% and 25%, which makes it incredibly rich, but I just need to temper that a little bit with some skimmed milk.

00:52:09.920 --> 00:52:18.280
I heat the milk until it is hot, but not boiling and slowly add it to the mixture in the bowl, whisking the whole time.

00:52:19.200 --> 00:52:23.840
Once that is incorporated... it goes back into the pan.

00:52:25.240 --> 00:52:32.440
Once the mixture reaches 70 degrees, I hold it there for five minutes to pasteurise the mix.

00:52:31.520 --> 00:52:33.160
The next stage is to cool this down as quickly as possible.

00:52:37.880 --> 00:52:40.840
I'm going to plunge it into a bucket of ice.

00:52:44.400 --> 00:52:47.400
So, here are the brioche loaves.

00:52:46.560 --> 00:52:50.240
They've come out of the oven and I've left them to cool on a cake rack.

00:52:49.480 --> 00:52:53.320
So when they are cold, I need to slice a couple of slices.

00:52:55.920 --> 00:52:59.280
You can see the mincemeat running through the loaf.

00:52:59.520 --> 00:53:09.600
Now, I've soaked the brioches in a mixture of egg, milk, cream, sugar, some walnut liqueur and some vanilla seeds.

00:53:08.720 --> 00:53:13.240
What I'm making here is, in essence, posh eggy bread.

00:53:12.360 --> 00:53:15.160
Put the brioches into the pan in which I have some sizzling clarified butter.

00:53:17.920 --> 00:53:23.600
It's important to do two processes to this, the butter first then the caramel afterwards.

00:53:22.800 --> 00:53:26.920
That way you get a wonderful crust that is also very delicate.

00:53:28.600 --> 00:53:37.360
The idea is the bread slices will be soaked with this wonderful creamy mixture, so it'll be semi-liquid inside.

00:53:37.560 --> 00:53:41.040
Once the slices are nicely golden, take them out of the pan and give the pan a quick clean with a dry cloth.

00:53:40.360 --> 00:53:41.680
Then add some unrefined castor sugar.

00:53:44.400 --> 00:53:52.440
I use this because it has low water content so it caramelises really quickly.

00:53:51.520 --> 00:53:53.000
Take the brioche slices and pop them back in.

00:53:52.200 --> 00:53:55.760
Be careful because this is very hot.

00:53:58.360 --> 00:54:02.760
Add a little more sugar on top of the slices and turn them to get an even coating of the golden caramel.

00:54:06.400 --> 00:54:10.160
Then drain your slices and put them on the plate.

00:54:10.640 --> 00:54:13.960
So, the next step is to butter the toast.

00:54:14.360 --> 00:54:21.480
I have got some brandy butter - a combination of butter, brandy and sugar.

00:54:20.760 --> 00:54:26.200
In here, I'vegot some mincemeat which we made with a range of dried fruits and spices.

00:54:25.360 --> 00:54:28.440
There is a bit of walnut in there as well.

00:54:27.560 --> 00:54:29.080
Just a touch of that on the side.

00:54:32.240 --> 00:54:39.760
So, there's the butter toast and the mincemeat and before I scramble the eggs, or scramble the ice cream, it wouldn't be breakfast without bacon, would it?

00:54:42.360 --> 00:54:46.840
So over here I've got some crystallised bacon.

00:54:45.880 --> 00:54:57.320
We've taken pancetta, frozen it, sliced it really thin - as thin as possible - and dried it in the oven so you get this wonderful, crisp, sweet bacon.

00:55:01.560 --> 00:55:06.960
So, there's the bacon on toast.

00:55:12.280 --> 00:55:15.960
I'm not scrambling them with heat.

00:55:17.040 --> 00:55:21.600
I'm going to scramble them with this - liquid nitrogen.

00:55:20.880 --> 00:55:32.880
The great thing about this technique is that because the nitrogen is minus 197-degrees centigrade, the ice crystals that form are tiny, making the ice cream really creamy and smooth.

00:55:35.920 --> 00:55:40.960
There we are, one nitro-scrambled reindeer milk ice cream on toast.

00:55:44.120 --> 00:55:49.800
Delicious.

00:55:48.840 --> 00:55:49.920
We do breakfast.

00:55:52.000 --> 00:55:53.200
And then we do lunch.

00:55:52.440 --> 00:55:55.480
It's beautiful toast!

00:55:54.520 --> 00:55:55.920
That's really good.

00:55:59.880 --> 00:56:01.360
It?

00:56:00.480 --> 00:56:02.320
This is amazing.

00:56:01.480 --> 00:56:02.680
Oh, that's very...

00:56:01.720 --> 00:56:02.840
It's so lovely.

00:56:06.520 --> 00:56:14.840
It was like the nicest glass of milk you've ever drunk at just the right temperature from the fridge.

00:56:13.880 --> 00:56:17.280
You know, when you have a chilled glass of milk that's just right.

00:56:16.520 --> 00:56:22.680
Sugared bacon.

00:56:28.200 --> 00:56:29.360
Wow!

00:56:28.400 --> 00:56:30.080
It's extraordinary.

00:56:33.160 --> 00:56:44.040
I don't understand how you can take... sugar and bacon and reconstitute them into a rasher that tastes that crispy and fatty, and yet...

00:56:43.880 --> 00:56:44.960
Oh!

00:56:44.120 --> 00:56:50.360
If you try to cook a brioche to get it that crisp and that soft in the middle...

00:56:49.480 --> 00:56:51.120
How do you do that?

00:56:50.840 --> 00:56:51.960
That's amazing.

00:56:51.080 --> 00:56:52.680
This is just lovely.

00:56:54.520 --> 00:56:56.680
But it was just delicious.

00:56:55.880 --> 00:57:00.480
Obviously the fact that it came all the way from Siberia...

00:56:59.640 --> 00:57:02.200
It doesn't land in your doorstep, ever.

00:57:01.480 --> 00:57:06.080
So, again, it felt like you were eating a one-off special meal that you'd always remember.

00:57:08.520 --> 00:57:12.040
That reindeer did not give up its milk in vain.

00:57:12.480 --> 00:57:16.280
For me, talking about a perfect Christmas, it can't be a perfect Christmas without snow in some shape or form.

00:57:15.320 --> 00:57:17.600
And...

00:57:21.000 --> 00:57:23.440
I needed snow.

00:57:24.320 --> 00:57:33.520
So the idea is I'm hiding behind a tree at the end of the meal and making it snow, creating snow for the end of my perfect Christmas dinner.

00:57:34.200 --> 00:57:35.800
Which is exactly what I did.

00:57:48.120 --> 00:57:49.920
It's snowing on Dara!

00:57:49.080 --> 00:57:50.280
Ah!

00:57:49.360 --> 00:57:51.840
Heston!

00:57:51.040 --> 00:57:53.040
Wow!

00:57:55.440 --> 00:58:02.680
I'm just excited to have eaten it, because you can see all the ideas going on and the boyish passion behind it.

00:58:01.920 --> 00:58:08.720
He's taken the component parts of a great Christmas and spun something magical.

00:58:07.840 --> 00:58:09.160
Truly astonishing!

00:58:11.440 --> 00:58:16.680
It really has been one thing, after one remarkable thing, after another.

00:58:15.880 --> 00:58:19.240
So it is very, very hard to take it all in.

00:58:18.800 --> 00:58:23.960
I've just got vague memories of different textures and tastes and reactions of, "Oh, what's this?

00:58:23.000 --> 00:58:24.280
Mmm!"

00:58:23.440 --> 00:58:24.600
Brilliant.

00:58:27.560 --> 00:58:30.280
That was wonderful.

00:58:31.200 --> 00:58:33.600
It was absolutely magnificent.

00:58:32.760 --> 00:58:33.920
Fantastic.

00:58:37.800 --> 00:58:44.440
I do feel like I opened the back of something and there was a golden ticket in it and we got to have this.

00:58:43.600 --> 00:58:45.800
This is insanely good.

00:58:45.920 --> 00:58:47.040
Really, really good.

00:58:46.120 --> 00:58:50.320
If somebody actually said to me, "How do you think it went?

00:58:49.560 --> 00:58:57.960
I'd have to say that I think I've just cooked one of the most memorable meals of my life.

00:59:32.480 --> 00:59:49.160
There's more to EastEnders this Christmas on BBC1.
